Here are my findings.

## 1. Business-day adjustment implementation

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BusinessDay.java`** — the core adjustment. Note it only rolls weekends forward to Monday; it does NOT skip holidays.

```java
// lines 1-18
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import java.time.LocalDate;

/**
 * Rolls a date forward onto a business day: a Saturday or Sunday becomes the
 * following Monday, while a weekday is returned unchanged.
 */
public final class BusinessDay {

    private BusinessDay() {
    }

    public static LocalDate onOrAfter(LocalDate date) {
        int day = date.getDayOfWeek().getValue(); // Mon=1 .. Sun=7
        return day > 5 ? date.plusDays(8 - day) : date;
    }
}
```

## 2. Callers / wiring (how it is invoked)

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java`** (lines 16-21) — the primary caller. It applies the adjustment to the owner's `registrationDate` (defaulting to `LocalDate.now()` if absent) during owner creation:

```java
public void service(@Valid @Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerMapper ownerMapper, Out<Owner> built) {
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(request);
    LocalDate date = owner.getRegistrationDate() == null ? LocalDate.now() : owner.getRegistrationDate();
    owner.setRegistrationDate(BusinessDay.onOrAfter(date));
    built.set(owner);
}
```

Two other callers use it to define "today's business day" as a filter key (not to store an adjusted date):

- **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RejectDailyLimit.java`** (line 17): `LocalDate today = BusinessDay.onOrAfter(LocalDate.now());` — counts owners registered on that business day and throws `DailyLimitException` (HTTP 429) at >= 100.
- **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BulkSignup.java`** (line 19): same pattern; warns when > 80 owners created on the current business day.

**Flow wiring** — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`. This is the OfficeFloor POST-owner pipeline. The relevant chain is `... -> rejectFutureDate -> build -> assignCustomerCode -> ...`, where `build` maps to `...owner.BuildOwner` (which invokes `BusinessDay.onOrAfter`). `rejectDailyLimit` earlier in the same chain maps to `...owner.RejectDailyLimit`. So `BusinessDay` runs as part of the owner-creation flow triggered by `POST /owners`.

Note: the update path **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ApplyOwner.java`** does NOT touch `registrationDate`, so the business-day adjustment applies only on creation.

## 3. Tests

There is **no dedicated unit test** for `BusinessDay` or `BuildOwner`. The only coverage is a black-box acceptance test plus a helper that replicates the logic.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** (lines 12-20) — asserts the invariant that a default registration date is never a weekend:

```java
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
